## 3.3. COMPONENTS [PART A]

* **Five main components** [A:16]:
  * Facemask [A:16]
  * Expiratory valve [A:16]
  * Corrugated reservoir tubing [A:16]
  * Fresh gas inflow tubing [A:16]
  * Reservoir bag [A:16]

### 3.3.1. BREATHING TUBES [PART A]

* Large diameter (22mm) creates low resistance pathway and potential reservoir for anesthetic gases [A:17].
* Volume of gas within the breathing tubes in most Mapleson circuits should be at least as great as patient's tidal volume [A:17].
* **Compliance considerations** [A:17]:
  * Compliance of the breathing tubes largely determines the compliance of the circuit [A:17].
  * Compliance is defined as the change of volume produced by a change in pressure [A:17].
  * 💡 Long breathing tubes with high compliance increase the difference between the volume of gas delivered to a circuit by a reservoir bag or ventilator and the volume actually delivered to the patient [A:17].
  * Example: If compliance of 8 mL gas/cm H₂O is pressurized during delivery of a tidal volume to 20 cm H₂O, 160 mL of the tidal volume will be lost to the circuit [A:17].
* These tubings connect one part of a breathing system to another and also act as a reservoir for gases in certain systems [B:5]
* They tend to be made of plastic, but other materials such as silicone rubber and silver-impregnated bactericidal plastics are available [B:5]
* The length of the breathing tubing is variable depending on the configuration of the breathing system used [B:5]
* They must promote laminar flow wherever possible, achieved by their being of a uniform and large diameter [B:5]
* The size for adults is 22-mm wide, while pediatric tubing is 15-mm wide to reduce bulk [B:5]
* The corrugations resist kinking and increase flexibility, but they produce greater turbulence than smooth-bore tubes [B:5]

### 3.3.2. FRESH GAS INLET [PART A]

* The position of the fresh gas inlet relative to other components determines the circuit type and efficiency [A:17].

### 3.3.3. APL VALVE [PART A]

* The APL valve is an adjustable, spring-loaded, one-way valve [A:17].
* **Functions** [A:17]:
  * Acts as a safety pressure limiter [A:17]
  * When pressure in system exceeds the set pressure, it allows gas to escape [A:17]
  * In the closed position, most APL valves have a pressure limit of 60-70 kPa to prevent barotrauma [A:17]
  * In the open position, it requires pressure of less than 0.1 kPa to open [A:17]
  * Generates PEEP when partially closed [A:17]
  * May or may not have scavenging attached [A:17]
* **Components** [B:5]:
  * The APL valve has three ports: the inlet, the patient and the exhaust [B:5]
  * The exhaust can be opened to the atmosphere or connected to the scavenging system using a shroud [B:5]
  * A lightweight disc rests on a knife-edge seating [B:5]
  * The disc is held onto its seating by a spring [B:5]
  * The tension in the spring, and therefore the valve's opening pressure, is controlled by the valve dial [B:5]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:5]:
  * This is a one-way, adjustable, spring-loaded valve [B:5]
  * The spring is used to adjust the pressure required to open the valve [B:5]
  * The disc rests on a knife-edge seating to minimize its area of contact [B:5]
  * The valve allows gases to escape when the pressure in the breathing system exceeds the valve's opening pressure [B:5]
  * During spontaneous ventilation, the patient generates a positive pressure in the system during expiration, causing the valve to open [B:5]
  * A pressure of less than 1 cm H₂O (0.1 kPa) is needed to actuate the valve when it is in the open position [B:5]
  * During positive pressure ventilation, a controlled leak is produced by adjusting the valve dial during inspiration, allowing control of the patient's airway pressure [B:5]
* **Problems in practice and safety features** [B:6]:
  * Malfunction of the scavenging system may cause excessive negative pressure, leading to the APL valve remaining open throughout respiration, creating an unwanted enormous increase in the breathing system's dead space [B:6]
  * The patient may be exposed to excessive positive pressure if the valve is closed during assisted ventilation [B:6]
  * A pressure relief safety mechanism actuated at a pressure of about 60 cm H₂O is present in modern designs, even when the cap is screwed down and the valve is fully closed [B:6]
  * Water vapor in exhaled gas may condense on the valve. The surface tension of the condensed water may cause the valve to stick [B:6]
  * The disc is usually made of a hydrophobic (water repelling) material, which prevents water from condensing on the disc [B:6]
  * The valve can add bulk to the breathing system [B:6]

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p4_img1_cbe7fb.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p5_img1_ab5e9d.jpeg)

### 3.3.4. RESERVOIR BAG [PART A]

* **Design:** Reservoir bags are designed to increase in compliance as their volume increases [A:17].
* **Functions** [A:17]:
  * Collects fresh gas during expiration, serving as a reservoir for the next breath [A:17]
	* Adult standard size is 2 liters [A:17]
	* Pediatric bags are usually 0.5 liters [A:17]
	* Up to 6 liter bags exist for special applications [A:17]
  * Visual monitor of spontaneous ventilation [A:17]
  * Manual support or control of ventilation [A:17]
* **Performance phases** [A:17]:
  * Three distinct phases:
	* Phase I: Initial filling to nominal 3-L capacity (adult bag) [A:17]
	* Phase II: Pressure rises rapidly to a peak with further volume addition [A:17]
	* Phase III: Further increases in volume result in a plateau or even a slight decrease in pressure [A:17]
* **Components** [B:7]:
  * Made of plastic (latex-free) or antistatic rubber [B:7]
  * Designs tend to be ellipsoidal in shape [B:7]
  * The standard adult size is 2 L [B:7]
  * The smallest size for pediatric use is 0.5 L [B:7]
  * Volumes from 0.5 to 6 L exist [B:7]
  * Bigger size reservoir bags are useful during inhalational induction (e.g., adult induction with sevoflurane) [B:7]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:7]:
  * The reservoir bag accommodates the FGF during expiration, acting as a reservoir available for the following inspiration [B:7]
  * Otherwise, the FGF must be at least the patient's peak inspiratory flow to prevent rebreathing, which may exceed 30 L/min in adults [B:7]
  * It acts as a monitor of the patient's ventilatory pattern during spontaneous breathing, serving as a very inaccurate guide to the patient's tidal volume [B:7]
  * It can be used to assist or control ventilation [B:8]
  * When employed in conjunction with the T-piece (Mapleson F system), a 0.5-L double-ended bag is used - the distal hole acts as an expiratory port [B:8]
* **Problems in practice and safety features** [B:8]:
  * Because of its compliance, the reservoir bag can accommodate rises in pressure in the breathing system better than other parts [B:8]
  * When grossly overinflated, the rubber reservoir bag can limit the pressure in the breathing system to about 40 cm H₂O due to Laplace's law dictating that the pressure (P) will fall as the bag's radius (r) increases: P = 2 (tension)/r [B:9]
  * The size of the bag depends on the breathing system and the patient - a small bag may not be large enough to provide a sufficient reservoir for a large tidal volume [B:9]
  * Too large a reservoir bag makes it difficult for it to act as a respiratory monitor [B:9]

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p7_img1_b435ef.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p8_img1_adf9a0.jpeg)

## 3.4. MAPLESON CIRCUIT TYPES [PART A/B]

* Three distinct functional groups: A, B+C, D+E+F [A:17]
* The relative location of components (APL valve, fresh gas inlet, breathing tube, reservoir bag) determines circuit performance and is the basis of the Mapleson classification [A:17].
* **General characteristics of groups** [A:17]:
  * A - Best efficiency in spontaneous ventilation (FGF 1x patient's minute ventilation); least efficiency in controlled ventilation (FGF as high as 20 L/min required) [A:17] [B:5]
  * D+E+F - More efficient than B+C (FGF 2.5x patient's minute ventilation) [A:17] [B:5]
  * A, B, C rarely used in current practice; D, E, F more commonly employed (i.e., Bain circuit) [A:17] [B:5]

### 3.4.1. MAPLESON A (MAGILL CIRCUIT) [PART A/B]

* **Configuration:** Fresh gas flow enters near the reservoir bag, APL valve near the patient [A:17].
* **Components** [B:11]:
  * Corrugated rubber or plastic tubing (usually 110-180 cm in length) with an internal volume of at least 550 mL [B:11]
  * A reservoir bag mounted at the machine end [B:12]
  * APL valve situated at the patient's end [B:12]
* **Performance during spontaneous ventilation** [A:17] [B:5]:
  * During expiration, alveolar gas containing CO₂ will be exhaled into the breathing tube or directly vented through the open APL valve [A:17].
  * Before inhalation, if FGF > Alveolar minute ventilation, the inflow of fresh gas will force remaining alveolar gas in the breathing tube to exit from APL valve [A:17].
  * If the breathing tube volume equals or exceeds tidal volume, the next inspiration will contain only fresh gas [A:17].
  * Because a fresh gas flow equal to minute ventilation is sufficient to prevent rebreathing, Mapleson A is the most efficient circuit for spontaneous ventilation [A:17] [B:5].
* **Performance during controlled ventilation** [A:17] [B:5]:
  * With controlled ventilation, the APL valve is partially closed [A:17].
  * No gas is vented during expiration as exhaled gas stagnates during the expiratory phase [A:17].
  * Very high fresh gas flows (greater than 3x minute ventilation) are required to prevent rebreathing [A:17] [B:5].
* **Clinical use:** Historically common but rarely used now due to inefficiency with controlled ventilation [B:5].
* **Detailed mechanism of action** [B:12]:
  1. During the first inspiration, all the gases are fresh and consist of oxygen and anaesthetic gases from the anaesthetic machine [B:12]
  2. As the patient exhales, the gases coming from the anatomical dead space (i.e., they have not undergone gas exchange so contain no CO2) are exhaled first and enter the tubing and are channelled back toward the reservoir bag, which is being filled continuously with FGF [B:13]
  3. The alveolar gases follow the anatomical dead space gases and enter the tubing [B:13]
  4. During the expiratory pause, pressure build-up within the system allows the FGF to expel the alveolar gases first out through the APL valve [B:13]
  5. By the time the patient inspires again, they get a mixture of FGF and the rebreathed anatomical dead space gases [B:13]
  6. It is a very efficient system for spontaneous breathing [B:13]. Because there is no gas exchange in the anatomical dead space, the FGF requirements to prevent rebreathing of alveolar gases are theoretically equal to the patient's alveolar minute volume (about 70 mL/kg/min) [B:13]
  7. The Magill system is not an efficient system for controlled ventilation. An FGF rate of three times the alveolar minute volume is required to prevent rebreathing [B:13]

* **Problems in practice** [B:13]:
  * The Magill system is not suitable for use with children of less than 25-30 kg body weight due to the increased dead space caused by the system's geometry at the patient's end [B:13]
  * Dead space is further increased by the angle piece and face mask [B:13]
  * One of its disadvantages is the heaviness of the APL valve at the patient's end, especially if connected to a scavenging system, which places a lot of drag on the connections at the patient's end [B:13]

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled.png_664389.png_8def9c.png)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p12_img1_dc4bdb.jpeg)

### 3.4.2. LACK SYSTEM (MAPLESON A) [PART B]

* **Design:** This is a coaxial modification of the Magill Mapleson A system [B:14]
* **Components** [B:14]:
  * 1.8-m length coaxial tubing (tube inside a tube) [B:14]
  * The FGF is through the outside tube, and the exhaled gases flow through the inside tube [B:14]
  * The inside tube is wide in diameter (14 mm) to reduce resistance to expiration. The outer tube's diameter is 30 mm [B:14]
  * The reservoir bag is mounted at the machine end [B:14]
  * The APL valve is mounted at the machine end, eliminating the drag on the connections at the patient's end, which is a problem with the Magill system [B:14]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:14]:
  * It has a similar mechanism to the Magill system except the Lack system is a coaxial version [B:14]
  * The fresh gas flows through the outside tube, whereas the exhaled gases flow through the inside tube [B:14]
  * An FGF rate of about 70 mL/kg/min is required in order to prevent rebreathing, making it an efficient breathing system for spontaneous ventilation [B:15]
  * Since it is based on the Magill system, it is not suitable for controlled ventilation [B:15]
  * A parallel tubing version of the system exists. This has separate inspiratory and expiratory tubing and retains the same flow characteristics as the coaxial version [B:15]

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p15_img1_774b45.jpeg)

### 3.4.3. MAPLESON B AND C (WATER'S CIRCUIT) [PART A]

* **Mapleson B Configuration:** APL valve and fresh gas flow both located at the patient end [A:18].
* **Mapleson C (Water's) Configuration:** Similar to B but with shorter, more compact tubing [A:18].
* **Performance:** Inefficient for both spontaneous and controlled ventilation [A:18].
* **Clinical use:** Mapleson C occasionally used in resuscitation but generally not preferred for anesthesia [A:18].
* **Components** [B:16]:
  * A reservoir bag. In the B system, corrugated tubing is attached to the bag and both act as a reservoir [B:16]
  * An APL valve at the patient's end [B:16]
  * FGF is added just proximal to the APL [B:16]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:17]:
  * Both systems are not efficient during spontaneous or controlled ventilation. An FGF of more than 1.5-2 times the minute volume is required to prevent rebreathing [B:17]
  * During controlled ventilation, the B system is slightly more efficient due to the corrugated tubing acting as a reservoir. An FGF of more than 50% of the minute ventilation is still required to prevent rebreathing [B:17]
  * Mapleson C (also known as the Waters circuit) is lightweight and compact. It is used in resuscitation situations as an alternative to a self-inflating bag [B:17]
  * By adjusting the APL valve, it allows positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) with a visual and tactile ventilation monitor [B:17]

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_1.png_84369c.png_99de70.png)

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_2.png_3900e2.png_4b1484.png)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p16_img1_877551.jpeg)

### 3.4.4. MAPLESON D AND BAIN CIRCUIT [PART A/B]

* **Configuration:** Interchanging the position of the APL valve and fresh gas inlet transforms Mapleson A into D [A:18].
* **Performance** [A:18] [B:5]:
  * Efficient during controlled ventilation [A:18] [B:5]
  * Fresh gas flow forces alveolar air away from patient and toward APL valve [A:18]
  * Requires approximately 2.5x minute ventilation as FGF to prevent rebreathing [A:18] [B:5]
* **Clinical use:** Common in modern practice, especially in the Bain modification [B:5]
* The Manley ventilator, which has been switched to a spontaneous ventilation mode, is an example of a non-coaxial Mapleson D system [B:18]

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_3.png_acd1d0.png_5691e4.png)

### 3.4.5. MAPLESON E (AYRE'S T-PIECE) [PART A]

* **Configuration:** No reservoir bag or APL valve [A:18].
* **Clinical use:** Primarily pediatric applications [A:18].

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_4.png_5cbeb7.png_3200d1.png)

### 3.4.6. MAPLESON F (JACKSON-REES CIRCUIT) [PART A/B]

* **Configuration:** Modification of Mapleson E with an open-ended reservoir bag [A:18].
* **Clinical use:** Commonly used in pediatric anesthesia [A:18] [B:5].
* **Advantages** [A:18]:
  * Low resistance [A:18]
  * Minimal dead space [A:18]
  * FGF required approximately 2.5x patient's minute ventilation [A:18]
  * Less gastric insufflation in pediatric patients [A:18]
* **Components** [B:23]:
  * A T-shaped tubing with three open ports [B:23]
  * Fresh gas from the anaesthetic machine is delivered via a tube to one port [B:23]
  * The second port leads to the patient's mask or tracheal tube/supraglottic airway device. The connection should be as short as possible to reduce dead space [B:23]
  * The third port leads to reservoir tubing. Jackson-Rees added a double-ended bag to the end of the reservoir tubing (making it Mapleson F) [B:23]
  * A modification exists where an APL valve is included before a closed-ended 500-mL reservoir bag. A pressure relief safety mechanism in the APL valve is actuated at a pressure of 30 cm H2O. This design allows effective scavenging [B:23]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:24]:
  * The system requires an FGF of 2.5-3 times the minute volume to prevent rebreathing with a minimal flow of 4 L/min, making the system inefficient [B:24]
  * The double-ended bag acts as a visual monitor during spontaneous ventilation. In addition, the bag can be used for assisted or controlled ventilation [B:24]
  * The bag can provide a degree of continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) during spontaneous ventilation [B:25]
  * Controlled ventilation is performed either by manual squeezing of the double-ended bag (intermittent occlusion of the reservoir tubing in the Mapleson E) or by removing the bag and connecting the reservoir tubing to a ventilator such as the Penlon Nuffield 200 [B:25]
  * The volume of the reservoir tubing determines the degree of rebreathing (too large a tube) or entrainment of ambient air (too small a tube). The volume of the reservoir tubing should approximate the patient's tidal volume [B:25]
* **Problems in practice and safety features** [B:25]:
  * Since there is no APL valve used in this breathing system, scavenging is a problem [B:25]
  * Patients younger than 6 years of age have a low functional residual capacity (FRC). Mapleson E was designed before the advantages of CPAP were recognized for increasing the FRC [B:25]
  * This problem can be partially overcome in the Mapleson F with the addition of the double-ended bag [B:25]

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_5.png_59e8e9.png_0a42aa.png)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p23_img1_f384e4.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p24_img1_22e2a1.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p24_img2_675c3d.jpeg)

## 3.5. FRESH GAS FLOW REQUIREMENTS [PART A/B]

* **FGF requirements by circuit type** [A:18] [B:5]:

| Mapleson Type | Spontaneous Ventilation | Controlled Ventilation |

|---------------|-------------------------|------------------------|

| A | 1 x MV | 2-3 x MV |

| B & C | 2-3 x MV | 2-3 x MV |

| D, E & F | 2-3 x MV | 1.5-2 x MV |

* 💡 These FGF requirements highlight why Mapleson A is efficient for spontaneous breathing but inefficient for controlled ventilation, while Mapleson D/E/F systems have more consistent requirements across ventilation modes [B:5].

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_6.png_f56224.png_b00b7f.png)

![Untitled](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_Untitled_7.png_099554.png_ac0510.png)

**Bottom Line:** Mapleson circuits are semi-open breathing systems classified by the relative positioning of their components. The arrangement significantly affects rebreathing and fresh gas flow requirements, with Mapleson A being most efficient for spontaneous ventilation and Mapleson D/E/F circuits better for controlled ventilation. Understanding these differences is essential for safe and efficient anesthetic gas delivery.

## 3.6. BAIN CIRCUIT [PART A/B] [COMP: PC]

Tags: #pA_equip #pB_C1 #pC_PC

Part A Reference: Anesthesia Equipment - Bain Circuit (Pages 18-19)

Part B Reference: C1 Airway, Oxygenation and Ventilation (Page 6)

Part C Reference: Procedural Care - Equipment Safety

### 3.6.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** Design and function [A:18], advantages and disadvantages [A:18].
* **Part B:** Clinical applications [B:6], safety considerations [B:6].
* **Part C:** Safety checks and procedural implementation [C:3].

**Exam Weighting:** Medium (Common specialized breathing system)

**Competency Focus:** [COMP: MK], [COMP: PC], [COMP: SBP]

### 3.6.2. PROPERTIES [PART A/B]

* **Design:** Coaxial version of Mapleson D [A:18] [B:6]
  * Fresh gas inlet inside breathing tube emptying into circuit at patient end [A:18]
  * Exhaled gases enter corrugated tubing and are vented through expiratory valve near reservoir bag [A:18]
* **Applications:** Used for both spontaneous and controlled ventilation [A:18] [B:6]
* **FGF requirements:** 2.5x patient's minute ventilation needed to prevent rebreathing [A:18] [B:6]
* **Components** [B:17]:
  * A length of coaxial tubing (tube inside a tube) [B:17]
  * The usual length is 180 cm, but it can be supplied at 270 cm (for dental or ophthalmic surgery) and 540 cm (for MRI scans where the anaesthetic machine needs to be kept outside the scanner's magnetic field) [B:19]
  * Increasing the length of the tubing does not affect the physical properties of the breathing system [B:19]
  * The fresh gas flows through the narrow inner tube (6 mm) while the exhaled gases flow through the outside tube (22 mm) [B:19]
  * The internal lumen has a swivel mount at the patient's end, ensuring that the internal tube cannot kink, thereby ensuring delivery of fresh gas to the patient [B:19]
  * The reservoir bag is mounted at the machine end [B:19]
  * The APL valve is mounted at the machine end [B:19]
* **Mechanism of action** [B:19]:
  * During spontaneous ventilation, the patient's exhaled gases are channelled back to the reservoir bag and become mixed with fresh gas [B:19]
  * Pressure build-up within the system will open the APL valve, allowing the venting of the mixture of the exhaled gases and fresh gas [B:19]
  * The FGF required to prevent rebreathing during spontaneous ventilation is about 1.5-2 times the alveolar minute volume [B:19]
  * A flow rate of 150-200 mL/kg/min is required, making it an inefficient and uneconomical system for use during spontaneous ventilation [B:19]
  * It is a more efficient system for controlled ventilation. A flow of 70-100 mL/kg/min will maintain normocapnia [B:21]
  * A flow of 100 mL/kg/min will cause moderate hypocapnia during controlled ventilation [B:21]
  * Connection to a ventilator is possible. By removing the reservoir bag, a ventilator such as the Penlon Nuffield 200 can be connected to the bag mount using a 1-m length of corrugated tubing (the volume of tubing must exceed 500 mL if the driving gas from the ventilator is not to enter the breathing system) [B:21]
  * The APL valve must be fully closed. In this situation it acts as a T-piece [B:21]
  * A parallel version of the D system is available [B:21]

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_BREATHING_SYSTEMS_image_5.png_dbecaf.png_9a7af7.png)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p17_img1_9b0353.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p18_img1_4ea4d1.jpeg)

![image.png](BREATHING_SYSTEMS_pdf_BREATHING.pdf_p21_img1_cc191f.jpeg)

### 3.6.3. ADVANTAGES AND HAZARDS [PART A/B] [COMP: SBP]

* **Advantages** [A:18] [B:6]:
  * Lightweight and convenient [A:18]
  * Disposable [A:18]
  * Allows scavenging of waste gases from APL valve [A:18]
  * Better retention of heat and humidity than conventional Mapleson D circuit [A:18]
  * Partial warming of inspiratory gas by counter-current exchange with warmer expired gases [A:18]
* **Hazards** [A:18] [B:6] [B:21]:
  * ⚠️ Unrecognized disconnection or kinking of inner FGF hose [A:18] [B:6]
  * If inner tube becomes disconnected, fresh gas flow escapes into the expiratory limb without reaching the patient [A:18]
  * Converting the entire circuit into dead space, causing significant rebreathing of CO₂ [B:6]
  * The internal tube can kink, preventing fresh gas from being delivered to the patient [B:21]
  * The internal tube can become disconnected at the machine end, causing a large increase in the dead space and resulting in hypoxaemia and hypercapnia [B:22]
  * Movement of the reservoir bag during spontaneous ventilation is not therefore an indication that the fresh gas is being delivered to the patient [B:22]

### 3.6.4. PETHICK TESTING TECHNIQUE [PART A/B] [COMP: PC]

* **Purpose:** Checking integrity of the inner fresh gas flow tube [A:19] [B:6] [C:3]
* **Technique** [A:19] [C:3]:
  1. High flow O₂ is fed into circuit while patient end is occluded until reservoir bag is filled [A:19] [C:3]
  2. Patient end is then opened and O₂ flushed into circuit [A:19] [C:3]
  3. If inner tube is intact, there will be Venturi effect at patient end (decreased circuit pressure, reservoir bag deflates) [A:19] [C:3]
  4. If inner tube is not intact, reservoir bag will remain inflated as FGF escapes into expiratory limb [A:19] [C:3]
* This test should be part of pre-anesthesia check if Bain circuit is used [A:19] [C:3]

**OSCE station mark scheme - Inspect Bain circuit** [C:3]:
* Visual inspection along length of tubing [C:3]
* Occlude the end, close APL valve and press O₂ flush [C:3]
  * Check bag does not deflate [C:3]
* Occlude the end, press O₂ flush to inflate bag, then remove thumb and press O₂ flush [C:3]
  * Bag should deflate via Venturi Effect [C:3]
  * This is Pethick's Test [C:3]
* Backbar pressure test [C:3]
  * Occlude inner tubing with 2ml syringe with fresh gas flowing [C:3]
  * Rotameter bobbin should drop as pressure backs up to rotameter [C:3]
* If all of the above complete, then system is **safe to use** [C:3]

**Bottom Line:** The Bain circuit is a coaxial Mapleson D system that offers practical advantages including lightweight design and improved heat/humidity conservation, but requires vigilant safety checks (particularly the Pethick test) to verify inner tube integrity and prevent CO₂ rebreathing.

# 4. OTHER BREATHING SYSTEMS [PART A/B]

